 uh in the lung  chamber these white cells are shown in  red here  uh change the bacteria and phagocytosis  just like what happens  in the living human law so we can not  only mimic the entire  process but we can also visualize  and analyze this very complex uh  process in real time at high resolution  so this was the proof of concept  demonstration of building complex organ  chip  systems published 10 years ago so  jumping in time  you might be wondering what's happening  now so one of the major trends in the  field of organs and trips is to develop  disease models and used these  specialized models for a variety of  biomedical applications including drug  testing  so just to give you a couple examples of  the lung disease models we're building  in my lab  as you can tell from this slide we're  actually developing one on a chip  system to study adverse health effects  of cigarette smoking  so we have a very specific goal of  investigating  what happens in small areas deep in the  lung during  smoking associated or induced  lung injuries and diseases so for those  who are new to this  this is what humans malaria tissue  typically looks like  the tissue consists of the columnar  epithelium supported by this  stroma containing stromal cells like  fibroblast  interstitial macrophages and also blood  vessels  so to mimic this multi-layered  architecture  we actually came up with this uh  multi-layered  microfluidic device consisting of five  layers  so this is work done by mark mondrinos  who is now professor at tulane  university  so to mimic this uh strumming layer  uh what we do is we fill this middle uh  chamber with  type 1 collagen gel mixed with human  lung fibroblasts  and this layer is then sandwiched  between these two chambers  uh lined with primary hemosphere  epithelial cells and lung  microvascular endothelial cells so using  the system we  culture especially this these area  epithelial cells under air to induce  differentiation this is a micrograph of  a 