e able  to model this injury induce the fibrotic  response  in this organ on a chip system  in the interest of time i'm going to  have to skip over this but we have we  studied also adverse health defects of  electronic cigarettes  using this system  so here's another uh example of lung  disease model we're developing in the  lab  so in this case we're modeling cancer  immunotherapy  for lung cancer so with the support from  the  cancer resistance research institute  we've been working over the past three  years  to engineer a system that would allow us  to mimic  immune cell cancer interactions  in the context of cancer immunotherapy  so in this project one of the first  things we had to do was to engineer  refusable human blood vessels and this  is the device that we use to do that  and uh this is a cutaway view of the  device so what's interesting here is the  fact that  there are these two barriers protruding  from the ceiling of the channel  so you use these barriers to trap liquid  in the middle lane  of this device so liquid is injected  into this middle lane it  nicely follows the lane it never spills  over  over to the set lanes because it gets  pinned along these barriers due to  surface tension  so this capillary pinning effect is  demonstrated in this movie where  black foot coloring is injected into the  central channel  there's a little bit of bulging but it  never spills over to the satellites  so using this technique we inject  hydrogel solution mixed with fibroblasts  and  endothelial cells and we  culture them in the hydrogel and during  culture  endothelial cells in this in the in the  hydrogel  assemble themselves into 3d vessels that  open to the side  channels so this vasculogenic process  is uh visualized in this movie where if  you look at this middle compartment  uh filled with uh red fluorescent entire  cells  over time you can see them develop a 3d  vascular structure in the hydrogel  scaffold  so this takes about five to seven days  and at the end of this  uh vasculogenic process uh the  endothelial cells  uh form these beautiful uh tubular  structures  and you can see these fibroblasts wrap  around these tubes  as if there are pericytes and these  vessels have open lumens as you can see  in this  section and we've also developed a  technique  uh to extract these uh vascularized  tissue constructs as a whole  after we formed them so these examples  can be further processed for imaging  this is a scan electron micrograph of a  single micro vessel  in the hydrogel scaffold we can do tem  so this is a tm image of a single  capillary which is very similar  to individual vessels and and we also  have the ability to tune the  architecture of  these blood vessels so depending on how  many cells you put in how many how stiff  the gel is  these are all the same type of  endothelial cells but they show very  different architecture  during culture and this micrograph  shows anastomosis of endothelial lining  in the side channels with the 3d vessels  in the gel  so as a result of anastomosis the  vessels open to the side channels and  the whole network  becomes perfusable as you can see in  this movie so this movie shows a  fluorescent  dye flowing through the vessels uh  and it's it's also possible to flow  microbes and nanoparticles for  for example targeted drug delivery  applications  uh we've also tested flowing uh blood  cells  red blood cells on the right on the left  and  t cells actually in this case they are  migrating uh  in the tnf treated vessels and you can  see all different sorts of behaviors  t-cell behaviors in this movie  so we're now using this as a platform to  build more realistic  3d human tissues so just to very quickly  walk you through some of the examples  we've developed the vascularized human  eyelid on a chip  system shown here where we  put these cadaveric eyelets actually we  receive from donors  into our device we vascularize and we  can maintain their function  for over a month