of of concept study  that we conducted so this micrograph  shows a mesothelion over expressing a549  tumor spheroids and we were actually  able to vascularize these tumors ferrows  in our device  so we're now using this uh model system  to study  uh the efficacy of engineered immune  cells like carties  so in this movie uh these red cells are  cartes  engineered to recognize a mesothelion  expressed by the tumor  spheroids this is a couple of hours  after infusion  as you can see many of these tumors  cartes  adhere to the tumor associated vessels  and within  just a couple of days many of these t  cells infiltrate the tumor and they're  found within the tumor  and as a result of this t-cell  infiltration  we see we saw significant effect on the  growth of tumors  and we also have evidence that t cells  induce  apoptosis of cancer cells to exert tumor  killing effects  so we're now using this system to screen  uh  the the effects of different kinds of  engineered t-cells and we're also  delving into the molecular biology of  tumor cancer interactions this is uh  probably one of the most exciting but  challenging projects  we've worked on in the lab so we've also  sent those tissue chips organ chips up  to space  we got selected as one of the five labs  in the country  about three years ago to send those  tissue chips up to  the international space station so in  this project we're  studying immunosuppression which refers  to this interesting phenomena that  occurs in the microgravity environment  so it's known that for many years  that astronauts get sick more easily  during or right after space flight and  there's  some evidence that the microgravity  environment  compromises our immune function so  we're actually investigating this  interesting uh  space phenomenon uh using  our model systems so in this project  we're actually  creating a model of respiratory  infection which accounts for  more than 30 percent of all infections  that occur in astronauts and we're 