ins the cells called the  cradocytes  these are normally quiescent stromal  cells  that live in the stromal tissue  and the conjunctival tissue contains  these goblet cells  that are responsible for producing mucus  which is one of the key components of  tears  another really interesting feature of  the ocular surface  is eye blinking shown in this animation  so as you can see here blinking x to  spread tears to form this thin  film on the ocular surface tissue  that serves to hydrate the tissue layer  and it also uh forms this a nice  smooth reflective surface for light  transmission so this is something we  found very interesting  from an engineering standpoint so to  mimic these  essential features uh we've created this  uh  micro engineered device that we call  the human blinking eye on a chip  so this work uh was done in  collaboration with the three  ophthalmologists at penn  uh shown here so this paper came out in  nature medicine last year so  if you're interested you can read more  about it and this is actually what the  entire device  looks like we use this uh 3d scaffold  highly porous scaffold  that looks like a context and so what we  do is we  to mimic these multi layered tissue  architecture  one of the first things we do is to  inject hydrogel  mixed with human primary human  carotosites into the cavities of the  scaffold  so this micrograph shows nice deep  penetration of the cells into the  scaffold  and we've also came up with the way to  deposit these two different cell types  in a physiologically relevant manner so  we can actually put down  corneal primary human corneal cells at  the center of the scaffold  and we can surround them with the  conjunctival cells to  form this concentric tissue pattern as  seen on the surface of the human eye  so during culture what we do is we first  to see the device we  keep everything immersed in culture  media we filled  the upper chamber with the media we flow  media  through the lower chamber as well but  once we form the tissue layer  confluent tissue layer on the surface of  the scaffold we aspirate liquid  from the upper chamber to expose the  cells to air  and we culture these cells at the air  liquid interface  to induce differentiation so during this  culture period  the cells are fed basically from the  media flowing in the fluorine chamber  so as a result of cell differentiation  as you can see here the corneal cells  that start off as a single layer  form multiple layers six to seven layers  and so basically they become  stratified they also form tight  cell cell junctions as you can see in  the sustaining of occluding which is one  of the  tight junction proteins and the marker  expressions so the cytokeratine 3 and 12  are terminal differentiation markers of  corneal epithelial cells  so as you can tell over time marker  expression increases but  it increases to a greater extent when  the cells are exposed to  air and these are more  uh interesting uh effects  or observations so initially the  cell surfaces are flat but as a result  of differentiation the corneal  epithelial cells form  these microstructures like microvilli  microplicae that are almost  indistinguishable from those features  from  seen on the surface of the human eye  we also confirmed that the some of these  conjunctival epithelial cells  during ali culture differentiate into  museum producing cells in our  device so once the cells are  differentiated  we use the system shown in this movie to  mimic blinking  so this is a gelatin hydrogel that we  use as a mimic  of the eyelid and we use this electro  electric motor to slide it back and  forth  to mimic blinking activities in  human eyes  so this is a close-up of the movie we  inject  contrived tears into this channel we  apply a little bit of pressure the fluid  comes out and gets picked up by this  sliding hydra eyelid and then gets  spread on the ocular surface  so the reason why we do this is to form  thin  film on the surface of the s