 over the past three years  or so  so uh hopefully you know i i was able to  convince you that organ to model systems  are great in that  they allow us to mimic the complexity of  human physiological systems  uh but the flip side is actually you  know it's not very easy to work with  these  systems it's a lot of work to develop  these model systems  it's also a lot of work to maintain  these tissue layers for long periods of  time  for experiments so for these technical  reasons  uh throughput experimental thrip and  typical organ ship  model systems is very low so i would say  this is  one of the most fundamental challenges  we as organ chip engineers fails  face in our field so we took this  problem to heart  and began to work on developing higher  throughput organs  technology and the way we do it is to  uh scale up by walking away from those a  single channel containing devices  and we have begun to use these plate  devices each containing tens of  replicates  and most recently we developed this uh  six inch wafer size device  that contains 128  individually addressable cell culture  chambers  which are regulated by very  sophisticated pneumatic circuits  and this movie shows individual  addressability  of these culture chambers shown on the  uh  left portion of this movie and we spent  a lot of time  on trying to automate the process of  manufacturing these chips  this device contains more than 2 000  holes and it takes about four and a half  hours to punch all these holes manually  so my student andre  who's leading this effort got frustrated  and he built this hole punching robot  from scratch  but we can also significantly enhance  the quality of hole punching as you can  tell by comparison  in this image so we also automate uh  fluid handling tasks in our model  systems  this is a robotic loading of hydrogel  cell mixture into the blood vessel  forming device  earlier so you can also automate uh  some of the key steps of cell culture  like media exchange so